1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,639
Chidi sent two hundred job applications.
He got zero replies.

2
00:00:05,559 --> 00:00:08,619
Not one interview. Not one email. Not even
a no.

3
00:00:09,539 --> 00:00:11,171
Chidi: Two hundred, Kiran. Two hundred.

4
00:00:12,091 --> 00:00:13,987
Kiran: And how many were the same CV?

5
00:00:14,656 --> 00:00:15,392
Chidi: ... Two hundred.

6
00:00:16,262 --> 00:00:19,059
Chidi is smart. He works hard. So what
went wrong?

7
00:00:19,979 --> 00:00:22,578
It was not his talent. It was what he
sent.

8
00:00:23,648 --> 00:00:28,029
In this video, I will show you how to find
a job in the UK, how to write a

9
00:00:28,049 --> 00:00:32,489
CV that gets replies, how to build a good
LinkedIn profile, how to spot the

10
00:00:32,509 --> 00:00:37,009
scams, and how to walk into an interview
with confidence. No shortcuts. Just

11
00:00:37,029 --> 00:00:37,961
steps that work.

12
00:00:39,281 --> 00:00:44,550
One important thing before we start.
Everything in this video is correct as

13
00:00:44,570 --> 00:00:45,749
of November 2026.

14
00:00:46,419 --> 00:00:51,966
Job rules and visa rules change often. So
before you act, check GOV.UK, your

15
00:00:51,986 --> 00:00:54,090
university, or your employer.

16
00:00:54,759 --> 00:00:57,658
Kiran: Rule zero. Check your own visa
conditions.

17
00:00:58,228 --> 00:00:59,699
Chidi: Mine are in very tiny letters.

18
00:01:00,469 --> 00:01:02,276
Kiran: Then read the tiny letters.

19
00:01:03,696 --> 00:01:08,215
First, the big picture. There is a simple
plan, in three stages.

20
00:01:08,886 --> 00:01:13,918
Stage one is a survival job. For the first
six months or so, you need money,

21
00:01:13,938 --> 00:01:18,970
a routine, and UK work experience. A
restaurant, a supermarket, a warehouse,

22
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:21,563
security. Any honest job is a good job.

23
00:01:22,232 --> 00:01:25,485
But it is a stepping stone. It is not the
finish line.

24
00:01:26,355 --> 00:01:31,114
Stage two is relevant experience. If you
came here to study, look for work

25
00:01:31,134 --> 00:01:34,989
connected to your course. Even part-time.
Even volunteering.

26
00:01:35,659 --> 00:01:40,313
Stage three is a job in your own career.
For many of you, that means a company

27
00:01:40,333 --> 00:01:41,931
that can sponsor your visa.

28
00:01:42,601 --> 00:01:43,741
Chidi: Can I skip to stage three?

29
00:01:44,562 --> 00:01:46,549
Kiran: It is stairs, Chidi. Not a lift.

30
00:01:47,068 --> 00:01:51,653
Nobody does this overnight. But the UK has
every kind of job. How far you go

31
00:01:51,673 --> 00:01:53,046
depends on your effort.

32
00:01:54,366 --> 00:01:58,417
Before you apply for anything, check your
own visa rules. This is the most

33
00:01:58,437 --> 00:01:59,242
important step.

34
00:01:59,912 --> 00:02:04,576
If you are on a Student visa, most full-
time university students can work up

35
00:02:04,596 --> 00:02:09,259
to twenty hours a week in term time, and
full time in the official holidays.

36
00:02:09,279 --> 00:02:13,819
If your course is below degree level, the
limit is usually ten hours. Your

37
00:02:13,839 --> 00:02:18,133
exact conditions are in your eVisa or your
decision letter. Read them.

38
00:02:18,803 --> 00:02:20,284
Chidi: Sixty hours a week! Easy money!

39
00:02:20,954 --> 00:02:22,694
Kiran: Chidi. Twenty. Not sixty.

40
00:02:23,464 --> 00:02:27,450
Going over your hours is not a small
mistake. It can put your visa at risk.

41
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:32,701
And here is one that catches students out.
You cannot be self-employed on a

42
00:02:32,721 --> 00:02:37,143
Student visa. That includes many gig jobs,
like delivery riding, where you

43
00:02:37,163 --> 00:02:41,884
work for yourself. Ask the company, and
ask your university's visa team, before

44
00:02:41,904 --> 00:02:42,484
you start.

45
00:02:43,254 --> 00:02:44,679
Chidi: I will become a delivery rider!

46
00:02:45,399 --> 00:02:48,062
Kiran: Self-employed. Not allowed. Ask
first.

47
00:02:48,832 --> 00:02:52,308
Paid work and volunteering both count
towards your weekly limit.

48
00:02:52,978 --> 00:02:57,560
If you are not on a Student visa, your
rules may be different. Always check

49
00:02:57,580 --> 00:02:58,112
your own.

50
00:02:59,682 --> 00:03:04,515
Now, the survival job. Restaurants, cafes,
supermarkets, warehouses, hotels,

51
00:03:04,535 --> 00:03:09,112
cleaning. They hire all year, and they are
a real way to pay your bills.

52
00:03:09,782 --> 00:03:14,772
Walk in with a short CV, smile, and ask
for the manager. Many jobs are filled

53
00:03:14,792 --> 00:03:15,358
that way.

54
00:03:16,028 --> 00:03:20,400
Every employer must check your right to
work. Use your share code, just like

55
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:21,440
in the last video.

56
00:03:22,110 --> 00:03:28,089
Check your pay. For age twenty-one and
over, the minimum is £12.71 an hour.

57
00:03:28,109 --> 00:03:31,449
For ages eighteen to twenty, it is £10.85.

58
00:03:32,119 --> 00:03:36,725
Security work is popular too. But you need
a licence from the SIA. That needs

59
00:03:36,745 --> 00:03:41,592
approved training, a criminal record
check, and a fee. Apply only through

60
00:03:41,612 --> 00:03:44,896
and check the current cost there before
you pay anyone.

61
00:03:45,666 --> 00:03:46,976
Chidi: Cafe job? I love coffee!

62
00:03:47,696 --> 00:03:49,734
Kiran: Good. Do it well. People remember.

63
00:03:50,454 --> 00:03:55,567
And give your survival job an end date.
Six months, maybe. Then move up.

64
00:03:57,136 --> 00:04:02,234
Now your CV. In the UK, a CV is one or two
pages. Not five.

65
00:04:02,905 --> 00:04:05,526
Chidi: Mine has my photo, my age, and five
pages.

66
00:04:06,096 --> 00:04:07,112
Kiran: We will fix that.

67
00:04:07,881 --> 00:04:12,424
At the top, put your name, phone number,
email, and your LinkedIn link. Do

68
00:04:12,444 --> 00:04:17,233
not put a photo. Do not put your date of
birth, your age, your marital status,

69
00:04:17,253 --> 00:04:18,466
or your nationality.

70
00:04:19,137 --> 00:04:24,178
Then, in this order: a short profile, your
work experience, your education,

71
00:04:24,198 --> 00:04:26,203
and your skills. Newest first.

72
00:04:26,873 --> 00:04:31,154
Under each job, show what you achieved,
with numbers. Not 'I worked in a shop'.

73
00:04:31,174 --> 00:04:34,801
Say 'I served around one hundred customers
a day', if that is true.

74
00:04:35,371 --> 00:04:40,781
And most important: change your CV for
every job. Use the words from the job

75
00:04:40,801 --> 00:04:41,281
advert.

76
00:04:42,751 --> 00:04:46,665
Good news. You do not need to pay anyone
to write a good CV. There are free

77
00:04:46,685 --> 00:04:46,980
tools.

78
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:52,203
The National Careers Service has a free CV
builder and guides. Google Docs

79
00:04:52,223 --> 00:04:56,327
and Microsoft Word have free CV templates.
Canva has free designs.

80
00:04:56,997 --> 00:05:01,976
Your university careers service is free
for students, and many will check your

81
00:05:01,996 --> 00:05:02,809
CV. Just ask.

82
00:05:03,479 --> 00:05:08,384
And you can use free AI tools, like
ChatGPT, Gemini or Claude, as a writing

83
00:05:08,404 --> 00:05:13,506
helper. Give it your real experience and
the job advert, and ask it to improve

84
00:05:13,526 --> 00:05:14,360
your wording.

85
00:05:14,930 --> 00:05:16,888
Chidi: Can the AI just write that I am a
doctor?

86
00:05:17,558 --> 00:05:18,860
Kiran: No. True things only.

87
00:05:19,630 --> 00:05:24,415
Be careful. Do not paste your passport
number or your home address. Check every

88
00:05:24,435 --> 00:05:28,430
line. And never let AI invent experience.
Every word must be true.

89
00:05:29,901 --> 00:05:35,327
Now, the dangerous part. People will try
to sell you a job, or a CV course.

90
00:05:36,196 --> 00:05:40,135
Mr Deal: Special CV course! Only five
hundred pounds! Guaranteed job!

91
00:05:40,656 --> 00:05:44,933
No one can guarantee you a job. And real
employers never ask you to pay to

92
00:05:44,953 --> 00:05:48,939
be hired. Not for training, a uniform, a
background check, or a visa.

93
00:05:49,708 --> 00:05:54,816
Slick Sid: I know a company. Pay me, and I
will get you a sponsored job.

94
00:05:55,337 --> 00:05:58,280
That is a scam. Jobs and visas are not for
sale.

95
00:05:59,050 --> 00:06:02,941
Officer Fake: Congratulations! You are
hired. Send two hundred pounds for your

96
00:06:02,961 --> 00:06:03,693
security check.

97
00:06:04,263 --> 00:06:05,623
Chidi: Guaranteed job! Where do I pay?

98
00:06:06,443 --> 00:06:08,039
Kiran: Chidi. Put the wallet down.

99
00:06:08,808 --> 00:06:13,581
Be careful with job offers on WhatsApp or
text, with recruiters who use free

100
00:06:13,601 --> 00:06:18,437
email addresses, and with offers for jobs
you never applied for. If a company

101
00:06:18,457 --> 00:06:23,230
promises sponsorship, check it on the
official Register of Licensed Sponsors

102
00:06:23,250 --> 00:06:23,861
on GOV.UK.

103
00:06:24,632 --> 00:06:29,989
If you are unsure, ask a friend. Free
advice is available from Citizens Advice,

104
00:06:30,009 --> 00:06:35,094
SAFERjobs and JobsAware. And report scams
to Report Fraud, the police fraud

105
00:06:35,114 --> 00:06:35,639
service.

106
00:06:37,109 --> 00:06:41,232
Next, LinkedIn. It is free, and recruiters
really do use it.

107
00:06:41,902 --> 00:06:46,511
Use a clear, friendly photo with a plain
background. Write a headline that

108
00:06:46,531 --> 00:06:51,454
says what you do, and what you want. For
example: Data analyst student, looking

109
00:06:51,474 --> 00:06:52,705
for part-time roles.

110
00:06:53,375 --> 00:06:55,110
Chidi: My headline is: Legend!

111
00:06:55,779 --> 00:06:59,654
Kiran: Try: Marketing graduate looking for
an internship.

112
00:07:00,175 --> 00:07:04,366
In the About section, write three or four
short sentences. Who you are, what

113
00:07:04,386 --> 00:07:06,583
you are good at, and what you want next.

114
00:07:07,253 --> 00:07:11,794
Under Experience, copy the best points
from your CV. Add your skills and your

115
00:07:11,814 --> 00:07:12,446
university.

116
00:07:13,116 --> 00:07:17,406
Switch on Open to Work, for recruiters
only. Follow companies you like. Connect

117
00:07:17,426 --> 00:07:20,953
with people in your field, and send them a
short, polite message.

118
00:07:21,523 --> 00:07:25,857
And remember, fake recruiters use LinkedIn
too. Never pay anyone who contacts

119
00:07:25,877 --> 00:07:26,423
you there.

120
00:07:27,893 --> 00:07:32,532
Where do you find the jobs? Indeed is a
good start. Make a free profile, upload

121
00:07:32,552 --> 00:07:35,894
your CV, and set job alerts for your area
and your field.

122
00:07:36,564 --> 00:07:41,686
Also use LinkedIn Jobs, Reed, Totaljobs,
and the government's Find a job service.

123
00:07:41,706 --> 00:07:45,622
Look at company websites too, and your
university's job board.

124
00:07:46,293 --> 00:07:51,089
Apply for jobs that fit your profile. Ten
good applications, written for each

125
00:07:51,109 --> 00:07:53,215
job, beat two hundred copied ones.

126
00:07:53,884 --> 00:07:55,141
Chidi: Ten? I sent two hundred.

127
00:07:56,061 --> 00:07:58,006
Kiran: Quality, Chidi. Not quantity.

128
00:07:58,726 --> 00:08:03,491
Keep a simple list. Company, date, link,
and what happened next. Follow up

129
00:08:03,511 --> 00:08:04,913
politely after a week.

130
00:08:05,584 --> 00:08:09,948
And if you hope to work here after your
studies, look at whether the employer

131
00:08:09,968 --> 00:08:13,251
can sponsor workers. Ask, and check the
official register.

132
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:17,005
If you came to study, this part is for
you.

133
00:08:17,676 --> 00:08:21,905
A restaurant job pays the bills. But a job
connected to your course builds

134
00:08:21,925 --> 00:08:22,594
your future.

135
00:08:23,264 --> 00:08:26,079
Kiran: Pays the bills now. Builds the
future later.

136
00:08:26,649 --> 00:08:31,820
Studying business? Look for work in sales,
admin or finance. Studying computing?

137
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:36,881
Look for tech support or data work.
Studying health? Look for care or hospital

138
00:08:36,901 --> 00:08:39,996
roles. Always within your visa hours, of
course.

139
00:08:40,665 --> 00:08:45,841
Internships, placement years, society
roles and volunteering all count as

140
00:08:45,861 --> 00:08:50,610
Ask your careers service. Many
universities have job boards just for

141
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:55,576
Why does it matter? Because your time
after the degree is limited. For most

142
00:08:55,596 --> 00:08:59,892
graduate visa applications from January
twenty twenty-seven, it is eighteen

143
00:08:59,912 --> 00:09:04,381
months. And a sponsored job usually needs
a licensed employer, and a role that

144
00:09:04,401 --> 00:09:06,280
meets the skill and salary rules.

145
00:09:07,050 --> 00:09:12,033
The experience you build now is what wins
the offer later. Check the details

146
00:09:12,053 --> 00:09:12,691
on GOV.UK.

147
00:09:14,261 --> 00:09:17,792
You got an interview! Congratulations.
Now, prepare.

148
00:09:18,412 --> 00:09:22,821
First, research the company. Read their
website, their news, their values,

149
00:09:22,841 --> 00:09:25,455
and the job advert again. Know what they
do.

150
00:09:26,124 --> 00:09:31,038
Second, prepare examples from your life.
For each one, say the situation, your

151
00:09:31,058 --> 00:09:33,252
task, what you did, and the result.

152
00:09:33,922 --> 00:09:38,812
Third, practise with a friend. Do a mock
interview, out loud, with real questions.

153
00:09:38,832 --> 00:09:40,549
It feels silly, but it works.

154
00:09:41,119 --> 00:09:42,239
Chidi: What if I forget everything?

155
00:09:43,158 --> 00:09:45,445
Kiran: That is why you practise. Out loud.

156
00:09:46,016 --> 00:09:50,266
Fourth, the day before, plan your journey,
or test your camera. And have your

157
00:09:50,286 --> 00:09:51,985
right-to-work share code ready.

158
00:09:53,355 --> 00:09:56,542
Now, the words you use. This matters more
than you think.

159
00:09:57,412 --> 00:10:02,002
Try not to say 'I guess', or 'I think
maybe', or 'sorry, my English'. They

160
00:10:02,022 --> 00:10:03,372
make you sound unsure.

161
00:10:04,042 --> 00:10:05,086
Chidi: I guess I am hardworking?

162
00:10:06,006 --> 00:10:06,854
Kiran: No guessing.

163
00:10:07,625 --> 00:10:09,815
Chidi: I managed forty orders an hour in
my last job!

164
00:10:10,284 --> 00:10:10,771
Kiran: Better.

165
00:10:11,491 --> 00:10:15,485
Say what you did. 'I led a team of four.'
'I solved this problem.' 'I believe

166
00:10:15,505 --> 00:10:18,978
I am the right person for this job,
because I have done it before.'

167
00:10:19,598 --> 00:10:24,220
But never lie. Employers check references
and documents, and lies are found.

168
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:27,519
A lie can lose you the job, and it can
hurt your visa.

169
00:10:28,288 --> 00:10:33,633
If you do not know something, be honest.
Say: 'I have not done that yet, but

170
00:10:33,653 --> 00:10:38,292
I learn fast, and here is how.' That is
confident, and it is true.

171
00:10:38,962 --> 00:10:41,947
At the end, ask one question. And say
thank you.

172
00:10:43,317 --> 00:10:44,887
Let us put it all together.

173
00:10:45,907 --> 00:10:50,665
Check your visa rules. Take a survival job
if you need one. Write a true, clear,

174
00:10:50,685 --> 00:10:55,682
tailored CV. Build your LinkedIn. Apply to
jobs that fit you. Prepare. Be confident.

175
00:10:55,702 --> 00:10:56,518
And be honest.

176
00:10:57,187 --> 00:11:01,673
Many students pay their fees and their
living costs by working part-time, or

177
00:11:01,693 --> 00:11:06,001
full-time when their visa allows. It is
hard. But people do it every day.

178
00:11:06,671 --> 00:11:12,120
There is no shortcut. No course, no agent,
and no secret replaces effort.

179
00:11:13,090 --> 00:11:17,726
But the UK has every kind of job. How far
you go depends on how much effort

180
00:11:17,746 --> 00:11:18,285
you give.

181
00:11:19,104 --> 00:11:20,435
Chidi: Two hundred applications again?

182
00:11:21,305 --> 00:11:22,458
Kiran: Ten. Good ones.

183
00:11:23,178 --> 00:11:28,632
One last time, because it matters. This is
general information, correct as

184
00:11:28,652 --> 00:11:34,475
of November 2026. It is not legal advice.
Rules change, so always check GOV.UK,

185
00:11:34,495 --> 00:11:38,174
your university, and your employer before
you act.

186
00:11:38,944 --> 00:11:44,337
Whether you came from Dhaka, Delhi,
Karachi, Kathmandu, Colombo, Lagos or

187
00:11:44,357 --> 00:11:47,043
you can do this. See you in the next one.
